1062 #uuid "296a64b2-a271-5186-bf37-e47d7b56624c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097653 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097653 #edb/ref 1061 27495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097653 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a032caf-6330-5e24-bafb-d45c348ad375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097653 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097659 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097659 #edb/ref 1061 27864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38143ed9-ca20-5c45-b750-274568e87032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097660 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097660 #edb/ref 1061 27865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097660 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a272f90-abf1-569a-853d-f9c7cfbd0534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097660 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097676 #edb/ref 1061 32551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f5e263b-c885-5894-bec5-5d0237b91689" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097747 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097747 #edb/ref 1061 45599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097747 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "97757635-74ae-5269-ac75-2ea85021adb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097747 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1061 4844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c93a74b3-e513-5fb9-9595-6bf177ec940a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097751 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1061 4845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82542d7a-3ec1-5979-8dd2-13a732ad5eb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1061 4849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13415159-b315-5389-bc81-61ef044820e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097753 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097754 #edb/ref 1061 4850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097754 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b70fcb39-7efc-50ad-a28c-73509579c61d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097754 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097791 #edb/ref 1064 "For a particle with position $x(t)$, the **average velocity** of the particle over the time interval $[a,b]$ is given by the average rate of change of position:\n\n$$\nv_{\\textrm{avg}} = \\dfrac{\\Delta x}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{x(b) - x(a)}{b-a}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the **average acceleration** of a particle with velocity $v(t)$ over the time interval $[a,b]$ is given by the average rate of change of velocity:\n\n$$\na_{\\textrm{avg}} = \\dfrac{\\Delta v}{\\Delta t} = \\dfrac{v(b) - v(a)}{b-a}\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that a particle moves along a straight line, and its position (in meters) relative to a fixed origin $O$ is given by the function\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t^3\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nt \\geq 0\n$$\n\nis the time measured in seconds. The average velocity of the particle over the time interval $[0,1]$ is\n\n$$\nv_{avg} = \\frac{Δx}{Δt} = (x(1) - x(0))/(1 - 0) = (2(1)^{3} - 2(0)^{3})/(1 - 0) = 2m/s\n$$\n\nLikewise, the velocity is\n\n$$\nv(t) = x'(t) = 6t^2\n$$\n\nso the average acceleration of the particle over the time interval $[0,1]$ is\n\n$$\na_{avg} = \\frac{Δv}{Δt} = (v(1) - v(0))/(1 - 0) = (6(1)^{2} - 6(0)^{2})/(1 - 0) = 6m/s^{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097791 #edb/ref 1065 1023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097791 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1c49e4b-7206-5819-b127-c2f562fa1043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097791 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097792 #edb/ref 1064 "So, we now know how to find\n\n- the average *velocity* (given the *position*), and\n- the average *acceleration* (given the *velocity*).\n\nBut what if we are given the *velocity* and asked to find the average *velocity*? That's a bit trickier. However, we can solve this problem by recalling that the average value of a function $v(t)$ over the interval $[t_1,t_2]$ is given by\n\n$$\nv_{\\textrm{avg}} = \\dfrac{1}{t_2-t_1}\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} v(t)\\,\\textrm d t\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097792 #edb/ref 1065 1026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097792 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b25ff39d-cfec-5109-8c49-31a8d54ad75d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097792 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Average Velocity Using Integration" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097796 #edb/ref 1064 "The region enclosed by the curves $y=x^4$ and $y=x$ is shown below. Suppose that this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the horizontal line $y=-1$, generating a solid of revolution. How can we write an integral expression that gives the volume of the solid?\n\n![](images/18/1880138e4a20cb5a47ba89cb3177d5dc6e74f998d7fdd428cdded7af38cadd02.png)\n\nFirst, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} &= x \\\\\nx^{4} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{3} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$.\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/49/49da0885bbf23e586b488e2b2a9bcaa0d923d3591833c9b8beb31a611c1d8b43.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=x^4$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $y=x$.\n\nNow, we can express the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves, as follows:\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=x$.![](images/35/352ff26bb5dce0a923c102c4528bd89555b49203c212fb73a42cf31cdf8b226e.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{outer} &= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(x) - (-1)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[x + 1]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=x^4$.![](images/ee/ee0fa31b61acd4a1f2a9b6bb21dcd0e5eed3c529780fcb67096d40dccf90c20c.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{inner} &= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(x^{4}) - (-1)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[x^{4} + 1]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$V|= V_{outer} - V_{inner}; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[x + 1]^{2}dx - π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[x^{4} + 1]^{2}dx; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}([x + 1]^{2} - [x^{4} + 1]^{2})dx$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097796 #edb/ref 1065 1064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097796 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8e33de60-9df4-540e-b19b-f12088b349e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097796 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097797 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose that the region enclosed by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac x2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt x\n$$\n\nis rotated $360^\\circ$ about the vertical line $x=-2$. Let's find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/78/7876211e2781a079ac2444436e671e2d61c28ec09774c0acad65e96fb537927a.png)\n\nFirst, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = \\frac{x}{2} & ⟹x = 2y \\\\ y = \\sqrt{x} & ⟹x = y^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y &= y^{2} \\\\\ny^{2} - 2y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y - 2) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y={2}$.\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/c3/c3fd84335240208fb158da9b67aaffa07cf490c67f8a77bf28947df998180b76.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x={y^2}$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x=2y$.\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=2y$.![](images/6a/6a075f9b5fb64923de7037b31ec6dd0408e88aa6d965ebb8b204b6c560e23ec1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{outer} &= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(2y) - (-2)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[2y + 2]^{2}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y^2$.![](images/cc/cca4c498956a16cb374a8d0b3341a09e52844026593557710525d07be79a233a.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{inner} &= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[(y^{2}) - (-2)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{2}[y^{2} + 2]^{2}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.Finally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$V|= V_{outer} - V_{inner}; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}[2y + 2]^{2}dy - π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}[y^{2} + 2]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}([2y + 2]^{2} - [y^{2} + 2]^{2})dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}(-y^{4} + 8y)dy; = \\left.π(-\\frac{y^{5}}{5} + 4y^{2})\\right|_{0}^{2}; = π[(-\\frac{32}{5} + 16) - 0]; = \\frac{48π}{5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097797 #edb/ref 1065 1066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097797 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "78a4b0ee-944d-5de3-846a-bf591d05387b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097797 #edb/ref 1067 "Rotation About a Vertical Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097798 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/d8/d8f08b1cc474cfee367ccf52e2299deaeec146ef7a7a860f64a75f7f7f25cb0b.png)\n\nConsider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^3-1, \\qquad y=2t-t^3, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)\n$$\n\nThe shaded area shown in the diagram is the finite region bounded between the curve, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines formed by $x$-axis and the points on the curve corresponding to $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1$, as shown.\n\nWe know that the area of the curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{a}^{b} y \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nSince our curve is defined parametrically, we need to change the variable of integration from $x$ to $t$ using the change of variables formula, given by\n\n$$\nA = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm d t}\\:\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nNow, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} - 1) = 3t^{2}\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y(t)\\frac{dx}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}(2t - t^{3}) \\cdot 3t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}6t^{3} - 3t^{5}dt \\\\\n&= 6\\int_{0}^{1}t^{3}dt - 3\\int_{0}^{1}t^{5}dt \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{4}}{4}\\right|_{0}^{1} - 3 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{t^{6}}{6}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 6(\\frac{1}{4} - 0) - 3(\\frac{1}{6} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shaded area shown in the picture has an area of $1$ square unit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097798 #edb/ref 1065 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097798 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1d0c890f-fbe0-5ded-948b-b94d13caac3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097798 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to *approximate*\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^x\n$$\n\nfor some value of $x$. We can do this using the second-degree Taylor polynomial about a point $x=a$. For example, the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $e^x$ about the point $x=1$ is shown below:\n\n$$\ne^x \\approx P_2(x) = e + e(x-1) + \\dfrac 1 2 e(x-1)^2\n$$\n\nIn general, the polynomial $P_2(x)$ is only an approximation to the function $f(x)$, which means that there will usually be some error whenever we use $P_2$ to approximate $f$. We want to know how bad this error can get. Can we find an upper bound for the error?\n\nLet $R_2(x)$ be the **error** (or **remainder**) that we get when we use $P_2$ to approximate\n\n$$\nf(x)=e^x\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\nR_2(x) = e^x - P_2(x)\n$$\n\nThe **Lagrange error bound** states that an upper bound for $R_2$ is given by,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.a\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(3)}(x) \\mid$ on the interval between $a$ and $x$.\n\nIn our case, since our Taylor polynomial is taken about the point $a=1$, the Lagrange error bound becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_2 & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{3!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.1\\right|^{3}\n$$\n\nNotice that the Lagrange error bound looks a lot like the last term of the *next* Taylor polynomial, $P_3(x)$.\n\nThe tricky part is finding the value of $M$. We'll learn how to do that in the following example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1065 1292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fcb4053a-c61a-538b-8b44-5a92f73f053f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097803 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1064 "The Lagrange error bound that we've discussed so far is valid only for the error associated with a second-degree Taylor polynomial. But we can generalize the result to higher-order Taylor polynomials.\n\nLet $R_n(x)$ be the error involved in approximating a function $f(x)$ by its $n$th-degree Taylor polynomial $P_n(x)$ about $x=a$. So, we have\n\n$$\nR_n(x) = f(x) - P_n(x)\n$$\n\nThen the Lagrange error bound for $R_n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}R_n(x) & \\leq \\dfrac{M}{(n+1)!}\\end{vmatrix}x-\\left.a\\right|^{n+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $\\mid f^{(n+1)} \\mid$ on the interval between $a$ and $x$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1065 1294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "07b10e6a-1673-5d6e-8572-8b4505579f44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097804 #edb/ref 1067 "The General Result of the Lagrange Error Bound" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097881 #edb/ref 1064 "We can also calculate the average position $x_{\\textrm{avg}}$ and average acceleration $a_{\\textrm{avg}}$ of a particle over an interval $[t_1, t_2]$ using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx_{avg} = \\frac{1}{t_{2} - t_{1}}\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}x(t)dt; a_{avg} = \\frac{1}{t_{2} - t_{1}}\\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}a(t)dt\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097881 #edb/ref 1065 5192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097881 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8e1cdc1f-de87-5b91-9ad0-f2bcab98be3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097881 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating Average Position and Acceleration Using Integration" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097915 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, the area between the $x$-axis and the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad y = y(t), \\qquad t_1 \\leq t \\leq t_2\n$$\n\nis given by the absolute value of the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} \\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When applying this formula, we assume that the chosen parametrization traverses the curve only once.\n- It's possible that this integral gives a negative value for the area even when the corresponding region lies entirely above the $x$-axis! To see why, note that if the curve lies above the $x$-axis, we must have $y(t) > 0$. However, if the curve is parameterized so that it is traversed from right to left, then $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0$, and we have\n$y\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} < 0$\nand thus, our integral turns out to be negative.\n- To ensure that we get an unsigned area, we take the absolute value of the integral.\n- In general, care must be taken when asked to compute the area bounded by a parametric curve when $y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}$ changes sign over the domain of integration. However, we won't consider that scenario here.\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097915 #edb/ref 1065 7915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097915 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "89e96b74-e406-52f5-b64e-46267df927e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097915 #edb/ref 1067 "A General Formula for the Area Bounded by a Parametric Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099909 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186099914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099909 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099909 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099909 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fac9b251-da24-5c85-a8c5-19a64644ffc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099910 #edb/ref 1080 "The series diverges" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099910 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099910 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a54e664a-f1d7-5bcb-8e94-1a6ce5022d23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099911 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099911 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099911 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "100c1476-f281-54e0-95a7-7818ad4fe29c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099912 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099912 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099912 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12064d86-564b-5b56-8f66-38d0279ac64d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099913 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099913 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099913 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "35745ab3-9c05-512d-aeca-22c906dffd62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099914 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099914 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099914 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43bdaffc-0049-5e1e-bbe6-abbbc04ef425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100901 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100901 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100901 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100901 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100901 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Volume When the Base Is Bounded by a Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100901 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100901 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e2e18711-6114-5c11-ad18-7b06bc0278d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100902 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100902 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100902 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100902 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing the Volume When the Base Is Bounded by Two Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100902 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100739 #edb/ref 17592186045